   286|<h1>Chapter 1 — The Artemis Accord</h1>
   287|
   288|<p>Mission AI: "Chapter 1: International agreement signed to establish first permanent moon base. All parameters nominal. Crew readiness: optimal."</p>
   289|<p>Elena: "They told us space was empty. They never mentioned how full it would feel."</p>
   290|<p>The regolith underfoot was finer than talc, sharper than shattered glass—a reminder that the Moon had known no water, no wind, no erosion for billions of years. Each step kicked up a plume of dust that hung in the airless silence before settling back to the gray surface, leaving temporary footprints that would last longer than any monument on Earth.</p>
   291|<p>Elena Vargas adjusted the grip on the sample container, feeling the unfamiliar pressure of the EVA gloves against palm and fingers. The suit's life support hummed softly in their ears—a constant reminder of the fragile bubble keeping them alive in this hostile grandeur. Outside their visor, the black sky was emblazoned with stars so bright they cast faint shadows, and there, hanging like a fragile ornament, was Earth—a swirl of blue and white that seemed both achingly close and infinitely distant.</p>
   292|<p>"Readings are nominal," Rajiv Patel reported, voice steady over the comms. Their boots crunched nearby as they monitored the portable analyzer. "Atmospheric processors holding at 10.2 psi. CO2 scrubbers at 96% efficiency. Radiation levels... actually lower than predicted. Something's different about today's solar flux."</p>
   293|<p>Elena Vargas paused, scanning the horizon where the solar array caught the sunlight like a field of metallic dragonflies. "Tell Mission AI we're proceeding with core sample Alpha. And Rajiv—keep an eye on those dust levels. I don't like how it's clinging to the suit joints."</p>
   294|<p>As they worked, the conversation drifted from technical to personal—a coping mechanism as old as exploration itself. Elena Vargas spoke of their daughter's first steps back in Spanish, of the way toddler gravity felt so different from lunar. Rajiv Patel admitted to recurring dreams of Earth's oceans, of waves crashing against shores they might never see again.</p>
   295|<p>The drill bit sang into the regolith, a sound conducted through bone and suit rather than air. Chips of lunar rock flew outward, catching the sunlight in brief, brilliant flashes. Each layer they penetrated was a page in the Moon's geological history—a history that, until now, had remained utterly unread.</p>
   296|<p>"Ten centimeters," Rajiv Patel announced. "Hit a change in density. Looks like we've breached the regolith into something... denser. Possibly compacted breccia from an ancient impact."</p>
   297|<p>Elena Vargas leaned closer, watching as the extracted core came free—a cylinder of lunar history, layers visible even in the monochrome suit lighting. "Not breccia. Look at this stratification. These layers... they're too regular. Too... deposited."</p>
   298|<p>A beat of silence, broken only by the hiss of life support and the distant ping of thermal expansion in the habitat's hull.</p>
   299|<p>"It's sedimentary," Elena Vargas whispered, the word feeling almost sacrilegious. "But that's impossible. There's been no liquid water here for billions of years."</p>
   300|<p>Unless, Rajiv Patel thought but didn't say, unless our understanding of lunar history is fundamentally wrong. Unless the Moon held secrets that would rewrite not just geology, but our understanding of planetary formation itself.</p>
