   288|<p>Mission AI: "Book 2 — Chapter 1: Deep space observatory array online. Long-wavelength cosmic observations initiated. Base status: nominal. Crew readiness: optimal. The next phase begins."</p>
   289|<p>Elena: "We learned to survive. Now we learn to see."</p>
   290|<p>The silence of the Moon had never truly been silent. Elena Vargas had learned that over the past six months—learned to hear the whisper of static from the base's instruments, the subaudible hum of the life support system, the faint crackle of cosmic radiation passing through the hull. But tonight, as she stood on the observation deck of the newly completed Deep Space Observatory, the silence felt different. It felt like anticipation.</p>
   291|<p>The telescope array stretched across the floor of Shackleton Crater like a mechanical flower opening its petals to the cosmos—hundreds of antennae arranged in precise geometric patterns, each one tuned to frequencies that Earth's atmosphere had been blocking since the beginning of life. Ultra-long wavelength radio astronomy, impossible from the surface of any planet with an atmosphere, was finally within reach.</p>
   292|<p>"First light in thirty minutes," Rajiv Patel said, his voice carrying the same reverent tone Elena had heard in cathedrals. He stood at the control console, his fingers hovering over the activation sequence as if afraid to disturb something sacred. "The array has completed its self-calibration. All four hundred thirty-seven elements are reporting nominal."</p>
   293|<p>Elena nodded, not taking her eyes off the starfield visible through the transparent dome. The Earth hung in its familiar place, a swirl of blue and white that had guided them through the long lunar nights. But tonight, she wasn't looking at Earth. She was looking past it—into the deep field where galaxies swarmed like fireflies and the echoes of the Big Bang still resonated.</p>
   294|<p>"We've spent six months learning to survive on this world," Elena said quietly. "Learning to grow food, to recycle every molecule of water, to protect ourselves from radiation and the vacuum. We've done what no humans have ever done before."</p>
   295|<p>"But surviving isn't the same as living," Rajiv finished. He stepped up beside her, his shoulder brushing hers. "And living isn't the same as understanding."</p>
   296|<p>"Mei was right, you know," Elena continued. "When she said we came to study the Moon, but the Moon was studying us. We've been so focused on what we're leaving behind that we forgot to look at what's ahead."</p>
