            p('Jonah leaned back in his chair, feeling the familiar tension between technological progress and philosophical depth. His great-grandfather Jonah Mercer Sr. had viewed salvage as desperation — using whatever worked because nothing better was available. His grandfather Jonah Mercer Jr. had begun to see salvage as calculation — balancing cost, availability, and performance in the endless struggle to maintain systems. His generation was fighting to understand that salvage wasn\'t just about reusing parts, but about values.'),
