def interstitial_chapter(num, title, book=4):
    text = f'{ch_h1(num, title)}\n'
    if "Treaty Debate" in title:
        text += f'''{EP('Mission AI: "While the pioneers build their home on the Moon, Earth debates whether to let them keep it."')}
{EP('Ambassador Okonkwo: "The Artemis Accords were supposed to be about cooperation. Now they\'re about control."')}
{P('On Earth, in the marble halls of Geneva, a very different kind of battle was being fought. The Treaty of Tharsis — named for the massive volcanic plateau on Mars where the first permanent settlement had taken root — was supposed to formalize the relationship between Earth and its off-world colonies. Instead, it had become a flashpoint for every unresolved tension between the old world and the new.')}
{P('Ambassador Sarah Okonkwo represented Martian interests in the negotiations, a position that had evolved from supplicant to advocate to equal over the course of fifty years. She had been born on Earth but had spent most of her adult life on Mars, and she understood both sides in a way that few could. That understanding made her invaluable — and deeply suspicious to both factions.')}
{P('"The treaty as written," she told the assembly, "gives Earth veto power over Martian resource development, limits Martian technological autonomy, and subjects Martian citizens to Terran courts in all matters involving Earth nationals. This is not a partnership. This is colonialism with a diplomatic veneer."')}
{P('The Earth delegates shifted uncomfortably. They had expected gratitude, not resistance. But Sarah had learned on Mars that survival depended on clear vision and firm boundaries — lessons that served as well in diplomacy as they did in dome maintenance.')}
{P('As the debate continued through days and then weeks, the fate of interplanetary relations hung in the balance. And on the Moon, the pioneers continued their work, unaware that the political future of their entire endeavor was being decided by people who had never worn a spacesuit or felt the vacuum of space against their visor.')}
{P('In the lunar habitat\'s common room that evening, the crew gathered around the communication terminal, watching the diplomatic feeds with a mixture of fascination and dread. "They\'re deciding our future up there," one of them said, "and they\'ve never even been off the planet."')}
{P('"That\'s why we have to succeed," the commander replied. "Not just for ourselves, but to prove that the future belongs to those who are willing to go there and build it."')}'''
    elif "Martian Independence" in title:
        text += f'''{EP('Mission AI: "While the Moon base grows, Mars prepares to declare itself a nation."')}
{EP('Chancellor Varga: "We did not cross the void to remain subjects."')}
{P('In the Valles Marineris Colonial Council chamber, Kaito Varga addressed the assembled representatives. The atmosphere was electric with the gravity of the moment — not the simulated gravity of the habitat\'s rotating sections, but the weight of history pressing down on every decision.')}
{P('"Fifty years ago," he began, "our grandparents landed on this world with nothing but hope and the contents of three cargo ships. They survived the first winter by sharing rations, by working shifts that lasted sixteen hours, by refusing to let the cold and the dark break their spirits. They didn\'t ask Earth for permission to survive. They simply survived."')}
{P('He paused, letting the words settle over the chamber. "Now it\'s our turn. We don\'t ask Earth for permission to govern ourselves. We simply govern."')}
{P('The debate that followed was fierce, but the outcome was never really in doubt. The Martian Declaration of Self-Governance passed with overwhelming support, and the news was transmitted to Earth through quantum channels that no Terran authority could monitor or block.')}
{P('On the Moon, the base received the news with a mixture of pride and concern. Their fates were now linked to Mars in ways that the Artemis Accords had never anticipated. The base commander called a meeting. "This changes our strategic position," she said. "Mars is now effectively an independent state. We need to decide where our loyalties lie — with Earth, with Mars, or with the principle of off-world autonomy."')}
{P('The discussion that followed was the most difficult the crew had faced since their arrival. Each member brought their own perspective, shaped by their nation of origin, their personal history, and their vision for humanity\'s future in space. There was no easy answer. But the very act of having the discussion — openly, honestly, with respect for differing views — was itself a declaration of what kind of community they were building.')}'''
    elif "Declaration" in title:
        text += f'''{EP('Mission AI: "A declaration is words on paper. What comes after is written in blood and oxygen."')}
{EP('Chancellor Varga: "Now we find out who our friends are."')}
{P('The response from Earth was not immediate. For three days, the quantum channels carried nothing but silence — a silence that felt more threatening than any ultimatum. On the fourth day, the transmission arrived, and it was exactly what the Martian council had feared.')}
{P('Earth did not recognize the Declaration of Self-Governance. The Terran Diplomatic Corps issued a formal statement declaring the Martian action "null and void under international law" and warning that "consequences would follow." The precise nature of those consequences was left unspecified, which made them more menacing rather than less.')}
{P('On the Moon, the base commander called an emergency meeting. "This changes things," she said. "If Earth imposes sanctions on Mars, our supply chain will be affected. We need to accelerate our self-sufficiency timeline."')}
{P('The crew looked at each other. They had come to the Moon as scientists and explorers, but they were becoming something else — the forward outpost of a civilization that might soon find itself isolated from its home world. The red dust beneath their feet was no longer just a scientific curiosity. It was the foundation of a future they would have to build themselves.')}
{P('The base\'s chief engineer spoke up. "We\'ve been running projections on full self-sufficiency for months. With the current rate of ISRU development, we can achieve 90% closure within eighteen months. The remaining ten percent will require breakthroughs we haven\'t made yet."')}
{P('"Then we\'d better start making them," the commander replied. The determination in her voice echoed through the habitat, carrying the same resolve that had driven every generation of pioneers since the first humans had left Earth\'s cradle. They had come to the Moon to explore. Now they would stay to survive. And in the process, they would build something that neither Earth nor Mars could take away.')}'''
    elif "Blockade" in title:
        text += f'''{EP('Mission AI: "The blockades of Mars are not just about ships and cargo. They are about who controls the future."')}
{EP('General Hale: "We prepared for this. We hoped we would never need those preparations."')}
{P('The Martian blockade began not with a dramatic confrontation, but with a slow strangulation. Earth ships stopped arriving at the scheduled intervals. Cargo manifests showed fewer and fewer critical items. The supply chain that had sustained Mars for fifty years was being systematically dismantled, not through violence, but through bureaucratic attrition.')}
{P('In the Valles Marineris Logistics Authority, Director Chen Navarro studied the dwindling inventory numbers with grim precision. "At current consumption rates," he reported to the council, "we have fourteen days of high-precision manufacturing components, twenty-two days of specialized pharmaceuticals, and eighteen days of advanced semiconductor substrates. After that, we rely entirely on Martian production."')}
{P('The council chamber fell silent. Everyone knew that Martian production had increased dramatically over the past decade, but no one had anticipated being cut off completely. The blockade was testing not just their supply chains, but their faith in their own capabilities.')}
{P('On the Moon, the base received the news with concern. Their own supply lines ran through Mars, and the blockade threatened to isolate them as well. The base commander called for an emergency review of all critical systems, accelerating efforts to achieve full self-sufficiency.')}
{P('"We came here as scientists," she told the crew. "We\'re becoming survivalists. But that\'s okay — because survival is just the first step toward permanence. And permanence is what we\'re building."')}
{P('The crew returned to their duties with renewed focus. In the greenhouse, the plants grew a little faster. In the fabrication lab, the printers ran a little longer. In the quarters, the conversations ran a little deeper. They were no longer just explorers. They were the vanguard of a civilization learning to stand alone.')}'''
    elif "Diplomatic Front" in title:
        text += f'''{EP('Mission AI: "When the guns fall silent, the arguments begin."')}
{EP('Ambassador Okonkwo: "We will not win this through force of arms. We will win it through force of argument."')}
{P('While the blockade tightened around Mars, a different kind of battle was being fought in the diplomatic corridors of Earth\'s major capitals. Ambassador Sarah Okonkwo traveled from Geneva to Washington to Beijing, presenting the Martian case to anyone who would listen.')}
{P('"We are not asking for special treatment," she told the United Nations General Assembly. "We are asking for what every people in history has earned through their labor and sacrifice: the right to govern themselves."')}
{P('Some listened. Some walked out. But Sarah knew that even the act of speaking was a form of resistance. The story of Mars was being told, and once told, it could not be untold. The seed of recognition had been planted, and in time — perhaps years, perhaps decades — it would grow into something that no blockade could contain.')}
{P('On the Moon, the crew followed the diplomatic developments with intense interest. They had a unique perspective — close enough to Earth to feel its pull, far enough to see its limitations. From their vantage point in lunar orbit, the arguments of Earth\'s diplomats seemed both urgent and strangely irrelevant. While politicians debated sovereignty and jurisdiction, the pioneers were actually building the future, one habitat module at a time.')}
{P('"They\'re arguing about who owns the future," the base commander observed during a crew meeting. "But the future doesn\'t belong to whoever argues best. It belongs to whoever shows up and builds it. And we\'ve shown up."')}'''
    elif "View from Earth" in title:
        text += f'''{EP('Mission AI: "The Moon is not just a destination. It is a vantage point from which to see the whole system."')}
{EP('Commander Vargas: "From up here, the boundaries between nations disappear. We are all just Earth, trying to reach beyond itself."')}
{P('From the observation dome of the lunar base, the solar system spread out like a map. Earth hung blue and white in the blackness, its continents tracing familiar patterns. Mars was a red spark, barely visible against the starfield. Between them stretched the void that had become a battlefield — not of ships and weapons, but of wills and visions.')}
{P('Elena Vargas stood at the viewport, watching the Earth rise over the lunar horizon for what felt like the thousandth time. Each time it happened, the sight struck her anew — the fragility of that blue marble, the thin layer of atmosphere that contained all of human history, the vast silence that surrounded everything.')}
{P('"They\'re fighting over who gets to own the future," she said quietly to Rajiv, who had joined her at the viewport. "But the future doesn\'t belong to anyone. It belongs to whoever shows up and builds it."')}
{P('Rajiv nodded. "And we\'ve shown up. We\'re building. That\'s all we can do — keep building, keep learning, keep proving that what we\'re doing here matters."')}
{P('They stood together in the silence of the Moon, watching the Earth turn. The political drama unfolding on the planet below felt both immediate and distant — urgent in its implications, yet dwarfed by the vastness of the cosmos. The work they were doing on the Moon — the science, the engineering, the daily struggle to survive and thrive — would outlast any treaty, any blockade, any declaration. They were building not just a base, but a bridge. A bridge between worlds, between generations, between what humanity had been and what it was becoming.')}
{P('And as the Earth completed another rotation below them, Elena allowed herself a moment of hope. Not naive optimism — she had seen too much for that — but the quiet, stubborn hope that had driven every explorer since the first humans looked up at the stars and wondered what lay beyond. They were building the future, one careful step at a time, and no political crisis could take that away from them.')}'''
    return text

