        EP('Jonah Mercer V: "We\'re not just recycling parts. We\'re honoring the work that brought us here."'),
        P('The alert came not as a crisis siren, but as a ceremonial chime: Salvage Yards Inventory - Millennium Milestone Reached. Jonah Mercer V adjusted his spectacles, the familiar weight of decades spent in recycled air and even more decades spent watching how humans actually treat their tools, as he looked at the notification flashing across his console in the Valles Marineris Fabrication Authority\'s central chamber.'),
        P('"Mission AI," he said, his voice carrying the rasp of years spent in workshops and the quiet pride of generational expertise, "confirm the nature of this milestone."'),
        P('There was the familiar micro-delay before the response came. The voice had logged every bolt tightened, every circuit traced, and every whispered conversation in the colony\'s fabrication archives for fifty years.'),
        P('"The Unified Salvage and Reclamation Network has processed its one-millionth salvaged component since the establishment of the first formal salvage yard. Current metrics show: 78% of all fabrication inputs now come from reclaimed Martian materials, average component lifespan has increased by 340% through systematic maintenance and upgrade protocols, novel fabrication techniques have been developed specifically for working with reclaimed regolith-smelted alloys, and the salvage economy now employs 12,000 Martians — 23% of the industrial workforce."'),
        P('Jonah leaned back in his chair, feeling the familiar tension between technological progress and philosophical depth. His great-grandfather Jonah Mercer Sr. had viewed salvage as desperation. His grandfather Jonah Mercer Jr. had begun to see salvage as calculation. His generation was fighting to understand that salvage was not just about reusing parts, but about values.'),
