        P('The salvage accords had begun as a practical response to scarcity. They had become something more: a philosophy of respect, a declaration that Martian civilization would not be measured by what it consumed, but by what it preserved. In every workshop, in every fabrication bay, in every home where broken things were mended rather than discarded, that philosophy was taking root. And in a world where every gram of material had to be accounted for, that philosophy was not just noble — it was survival.'),
